1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,819
Remember that time Cartman said this absolute nonsense?

2
00:00:03,520 --> 00:00:07,639
If you eat food and crap out your butt, then maybe if you stuck food in your butt,

3
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:08,660
you would crap out your mouth.

4
00:00:09,179 --> 00:00:12,269
And then to everyone's shock and surprise, this happened.

5
00:00:13,039 --> 00:00:22,899
Gross! But listen, South Park's most controversial character has claimed many delusional things over the years,

6
00:00:23,280 --> 00:00:25,879
but sometimes he's actually been spot on.

7
00:00:26,190 --> 00:00:31,539
So let's look at all the times Cartman said something totally outrageous, only to find out it was true.

8
00:00:32,020 --> 00:00:36,039
Starting with the time he predicted stem cells could be used to create a pizza joint.

9
00:00:36,640 --> 00:00:38,640
Let the lunacy begin.

10
00:00:39,020 --> 00:00:44,380
The episode Kenny Dies starts off with the show explaining what stem cells are in its own special way.

11
00:00:44,579 --> 00:00:46,899
And if you sign a release, that tissue can go to study.

12
00:00:47,100 --> 00:00:49,200
Oh, well, I suppose it can help others.

13
00:00:49,560 --> 00:00:50,700
The hope is that someday it might.

14
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:54,159
Right now, a lot of stem cells come from the tissue of aborted fetuses.

15
00:00:54,500 --> 00:00:55,899
I'm sorry, unwanted children.

16
00:00:56,380 --> 00:01:01,799
ACEs. What follows is a little sequence showing how the stem cells are stored and then transported to the research lab.

17
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:04,840
But unfortunately, this batch doesn't seem to make it to its destination.

18
00:01:12,849 --> 00:01:14,200
Well, that's unfortunate.

19
00:01:14,540 --> 00:01:17,099
Even more so as the first person to rock up at the crash site.

20
00:01:17,920 --> 00:01:19,260
What do we have now?

21
00:01:20,799 --> 00:01:21,060
Come on.

22
00:01:22,939 --> 00:01:27,219
Yes, the maniacal little hamster living inside Cartman's brain is already spinning like crazy,

23
00:01:27,280 --> 00:01:29,760
and it doesn't take long before he reveals his plan to the gang.

24
00:01:29,980 --> 00:01:33,060
With all the research that's going on, stem cells are worth three times their weight in gold.

25
00:01:33,280 --> 00:01:35,579
What I have in my backyard is an aborted treasure chest.

26
00:01:36,260 --> 00:01:37,099
I hate you so much.

27
00:01:40,480 --> 00:01:43,980
Try selling it to the same people who lost their order on that truck that crashed.

28
00:01:44,159 --> 00:01:46,299
Unfortunately for Cartman, and also the real world,

29
00:01:46,620 --> 00:01:49,879
scientific research can be shut down faster than you can say political mumbo jumbo.

30
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:53,459
I'm afraid the government has just put a ban on stem cell research.

31
00:01:53,859 --> 00:01:55,340
We really could have helped a lot of people.

32
00:01:57,180 --> 00:01:59,680
Hi! What the hell am I supposed to do with these things, huh?

33
00:02:00,060 --> 00:02:02,540
A bit late for that kind of rational thinking now, my dude.

34
00:02:02,680 --> 00:02:07,159
While Cartman laments his self-inflicted dilemma of what to do with 33 fetuses he stole from a crash site,

35
00:02:07,299 --> 00:02:09,240
the boys get some terrible news.

36
00:02:09,400 --> 00:02:13,580
Children, Kenny has been diagnosed with a terminal disease.

37
00:02:14,280 --> 00:02:15,860
The doctors are gonna make him better, right?

38
00:02:16,180 --> 00:02:17,819
Mom, that's what hospitals are for.

39
00:02:17,939 --> 00:02:18,780
They can make him better.

40
00:02:19,139 --> 00:02:20,340
Oh dear, Mandy.

41
00:02:20,939 --> 00:02:23,780
We know what that means, and it hits the boys hard.

42
00:02:24,219 --> 00:02:24,979
Especially Stan.

43
00:02:25,419 --> 00:02:28,689
They go and tell Cartman, and the three of them visit Kenny in the hospital.

44
00:02:29,020 --> 00:02:29,939
Hey dude, how's it going?

45
00:02:31,219 --> 00:02:32,400
We brought you a present.

46
00:02:32,699 --> 00:02:33,719
It's a gobo fighter.

47
00:02:34,300 --> 00:02:35,740
Don't tell him what it is, dude.

48
00:02:35,900 --> 00:02:38,560
Sorry. Good golly, goober.

49
00:02:38,740 --> 00:02:40,379
This is so sad, I can't bear it.

50
00:02:40,560 --> 00:02:41,460
And neither can Stan.

51
00:02:41,860 --> 00:02:42,800
Even Cartman is cracking.

52
00:02:43,439 --> 00:02:46,139
So much so that he sets out to see if he can find a cure for Kenny.

53
00:02:46,340 --> 00:02:48,500
Doctor, can you tell me exactly how stem cells work?

54
00:02:48,780 --> 00:02:51,099
Heart cells, skin cells, brain cells, and so on.

55
00:02:51,180 --> 00:02:52,979
Look, you have trillions of cells in your body.

56
00:02:53,099 --> 00:02:54,560
It's what we call a stem cell.

57
00:02:54,840 --> 00:03:00,020
But before a cell is designated as a toenail cell or a pancreas cell, sort of like a blank cell,

58
00:03:00,139 --> 00:03:00,389
do you understand?

59
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:05,099
Cartman does not, but he wants to, so he urges the researcher to explain some more.

60
00:03:05,159 --> 00:03:08,080
Earlier this year, we surgically removed the middle of her spine.

61
00:03:08,180 --> 00:03:12,120
Then we injected her with some stem cells, and they became the missing part of the spine.

62
00:03:12,439 --> 00:03:14,840
Yeah, pretty incredible, and Cartman is starting to get it.

63
00:03:15,240 --> 00:03:18,259
Could stem cells be used on someone whose muscle tissue is wasting away?

64
00:03:18,419 --> 00:03:22,819
If the stem cells were placed next to a Shakey's Pizza, they would become another Shakey's Pizza!

65
00:03:22,919 --> 00:03:25,719
Naturally, the researcher doesn't think this is a very good practical idea,

66
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:30,400
but says that we'll never know the answer to Cartman's insane question because the governmental ban on stem cell research.

67
00:03:31,240 --> 00:03:35,159
As if things like bans and bureaucracy have ever stopped Cartman before.

68
00:03:35,620 --> 00:03:39,080
And so he marches straight to Congress and pleads in front of the H.O.R.

69
00:03:39,159 --> 00:03:41,780
to allow stem cell research so he can save his best friend.

70
00:03:42,020 --> 00:03:46,080
I realize that using the tissue from aborted fetuses for research is a touchy subject,

71
00:03:46,139 --> 00:03:48,740
but stem cell research could save my best friend's life.

72
00:03:48,780 --> 00:03:52,280
Well, I guess I owe it to him to try and change your minds.

73
00:03:53,620 --> 00:03:53,900
It's touching.

74
00:03:54,400 --> 00:03:56,219
So touching that it makes Cartman lose it again,

75
00:03:56,240 --> 00:03:59,979
but he quickly composes himself by singing Heat of the Moment for some inexplicable reason.

76
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:02,460
And soon, the whole of Congress is swept up by the sheer madness.

77
00:04:02,780 --> 00:04:05,419
One thing that to another, we were young!

78
00:04:06,400 --> 00:04:09,310
If that ever happened in real life, the world would implode.

79
00:04:09,580 --> 00:04:13,000
Also, the more I think about it, the more that song choice absolutely makes sense,

80
00:04:13,060 --> 00:04:16,920
but I'm not going to delve into a philosophical discussion about actions and consequences right now.

81
00:04:17,339 --> 00:04:22,459
What's important is that Cartman got the ban lifted by getting Congress to Kumbaya to an 80s progressive rock song.

82
00:04:22,759 --> 00:04:25,769
And so he goes to Planned Parenthood to go get himself some fetuses.

83
00:04:26,259 --> 00:04:29,339
Great. Listen, if you'd like to sell your abortion for research, I'm offering $75.

84
00:04:29,639 --> 00:04:31,139
Help you out a little bit with your medical bill in there.

85
00:04:31,199 --> 00:04:31,899
Yeah, just sign right here.

86
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:32,779
Uh, excuse me, ma'am.

87
00:04:32,939 --> 00:04:34,360
Can I interest you in selling your aborted fetus?

88
00:04:34,660 --> 00:04:35,920
That pricker just doesn't quit.

89
00:04:36,319 --> 00:04:39,129
Unfortunately, it's all too late for Kumbaya and he dies.

90
00:04:39,379 --> 00:04:41,800
Again. Everyone's super sad during the funeral.

91
00:04:41,819 --> 00:04:43,579
That is, everyone except for Cartman.

92
00:04:44,139 --> 00:04:45,379
You guys, come quick!

93
00:04:45,579 --> 00:04:46,199
What, Cartman?

94
00:04:46,319 --> 00:04:47,660
Why, it's a miracle, you guys!

95
00:04:49,959 --> 00:04:50,839
What, Cartman, what's happened?

96
00:04:51,100 --> 00:04:56,779
Look, I put the stem cells from all the fetuses I had next to Shakey's and they are replicating a new Shakey's!

97
00:04:57,180 --> 00:04:57,500
It worked!

98
00:04:57,639 --> 00:04:58,199
Uh-huh.

99
00:04:58,699 --> 00:04:59,339
He was right.

100
00:04:59,699 --> 00:05:01,560
It can, in fact, be done.

101
00:05:02,240 --> 00:05:03,459
He never wanted to help, Kenny.

102
00:05:03,680 --> 00:05:07,180
He just wanted to build his own pizza joint using stem cells like he said he could.

103
00:05:07,360 --> 00:05:09,819
100 or so more aborted babies and I can finish up the kitchen.

104
00:05:13,279 --> 00:05:15,560
Hey, I wasn't Kenny's worst f-f-f-friend.

105
00:05:15,779 --> 00:05:16,420
Cartman was.

106
00:05:16,800 --> 00:05:18,160
Beat him into the hospital, Kyle.

107
00:05:18,379 --> 00:05:22,920
The next episode in which Cartman has proven to be right involves one of his many entrepreneurial endeavors over the years.

108
00:05:23,439 --> 00:05:24,000
And poop.

109
00:05:24,600 --> 00:05:28,410
The episode My Future Self and Me kicks off with the boys discovering a joint in the woods.

110
00:05:28,620 --> 00:05:32,519
They're totally freaked out by it and think that even just touching it might cause them to either start shooting

111
00:05:32,519 --> 00:05:36,500
up heroin or become full-blown terrorists because anti-marijuana ads can be crazy sometimes.

112
00:05:36,920 --> 00:05:42,220
Stan is the only one with a brain so he throws it away but that night an unexpected visitor arrives at the Marshes.

113
00:05:42,420 --> 00:05:42,839
Who are you?

114
00:05:43,459 --> 00:05:44,240
It's me, Mom!

115
00:05:44,399 --> 00:05:45,259
Your son, Stan!

116
00:05:45,680 --> 00:05:46,480
Okay, weird man.

117
00:05:46,819 --> 00:05:50,399
Stan's parents try to get rid of him but he convinces them to just hear him out by sharing things

118
00:05:50,399 --> 00:05:51,959
only the Marsh family would know.

119
00:05:52,180 --> 00:05:56,639
When I was five and you both found that squirrel I'd been keeping in the closet and then it ran

120
00:05:56,639 --> 00:05:58,779
away but I don't know that's just what you told me.

121
00:05:58,839 --> 00:06:00,540
I knew Mom actually let it out.

122
00:06:01,040 --> 00:06:02,519
He is our son from the future.

123
00:06:02,759 --> 00:06:03,670
You don't say weirdo.

124
00:06:04,040 --> 00:06:08,100
Little Stan immediately thinks his future self is so messed up because he touched that joint and his parents think

125
00:06:08,100 --> 00:06:13,040
all this means the bum has to move in with them and hang out with their young son every second of the day.

126
00:06:13,480 --> 00:06:15,740
Questionable logic all around but that's what happens.

127
00:06:16,240 --> 00:06:20,879
Soon Stan learns that Butters also has a future self living with him which naturally raises some suspicion.

128
00:06:21,160 --> 00:06:22,439
See here he is, future Butters.

129
00:06:22,920 --> 00:06:24,160
Future self this is my good friend.

130
00:06:24,240 --> 00:06:26,300
Stan! Sure I remember you!

131
00:06:26,459 --> 00:06:28,540
Yep, wearing that puffball hat like always!

132
00:06:28,819 --> 00:06:36,699
Yeah, something's definitely smelling like bullfarts and Stan's not going to hang around and let this nonsense continue indefinitely so he does himself some investigation.

133
00:06:37,279 --> 00:06:37,579
Here it is.

134
00:06:38,399 --> 00:06:39,160
There has to be something...

135
00:06:39,439 --> 00:06:40,199
Wait, what's this?

136
00:06:40,529 --> 00:06:42,100
This expires in 2002.

137
00:06:42,339 --> 00:06:43,279
Why would he have this in his wallet?

138
00:06:43,699 --> 00:06:45,240
3451 Colfax Avenue.

139
00:06:45,399 --> 00:06:46,019
Come on Butters!

140
00:06:46,199 --> 00:06:47,480
Are we out for an adventure?

141
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:53,639
Well I guess we're all going to 3451 Colfax Avenue the location of Motivation Core where Stan and Butters learned

142
00:06:53,639 --> 00:06:56,139
the truth behind two bozos suddenly living in their houses.

143
00:06:56,560 --> 00:06:58,720
We just don't know how to talk to our son about drugs.

144
00:06:59,079 --> 00:07:00,259
Well now you won't have to.

145
00:07:00,500 --> 00:07:01,519
This is Josh Kashner.

146
00:07:01,759 --> 00:07:03,379
He'll be playing the role of your future son.

147
00:07:03,480 --> 00:07:05,180
Nice to meet you, Mom and Dad.

148
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:11,839
Yep, these supposed future self adults are all just actors hired to scare kids into staying away from any and

149
00:07:11,839 --> 00:07:14,079
all substances because their parents don't want a parent.

150
00:07:14,209 --> 00:07:18,920
I'm going to tell him that I dropped out of school and went to prison for 8 years where I was sodomized.

151
00:07:19,639 --> 00:07:20,300
In the ass!

152
00:07:20,740 --> 00:07:22,740
Woohoo! That should get Kevin to stay clear of drugs.

153
00:07:22,920 --> 00:07:27,500
Again, the degree of logic here is astounding but you know the adults at South Park have a collective IQ

154
00:07:27,540 --> 00:07:30,199
of a cabbage so it's not really all that surprising.

155
00:07:30,720 --> 00:07:34,199
Stan of course is livid and so is Butters when he finally gets what's going on.

156
00:07:34,560 --> 00:07:39,160
They want their revenge and conveniently stumble upon something called the Parental Revenge Center.

157
00:07:39,699 --> 00:07:40,220
Uh, hello?

158
00:07:40,360 --> 00:07:41,920
We're here about revenge on our parents?

159
00:07:42,199 --> 00:07:43,060
Guys, come in please.

160
00:07:43,459 --> 00:07:46,069
Cartman? Oh, Stan, Butters, I didn't know it was you guys.

161
00:07:46,360 --> 00:07:47,300
What the hell are you doing here?

162
00:07:47,560 --> 00:07:48,480
I'm running a business, Stan.

163
00:07:48,579 --> 00:07:49,339
Are you my 11 o'clock?

164
00:07:49,500 --> 00:07:54,319
Of course he's running a business to get back at parents because Cartman has no time for any authority except his own.

165
00:07:54,459 --> 00:07:57,319
He pitches his business to Stan and he actually makes some pretty good points.

166
00:07:57,600 --> 00:08:00,279
They need to see consequences from their actions or else they'll never learn.

167
00:08:00,360 --> 00:08:02,660
Listen, parents understand one thing and that's consequences.

168
00:08:03,240 --> 00:08:07,639
What my company does is inflicts those consequences upon the parents in a very real and very direct way.

169
00:08:07,800 --> 00:08:12,040
So it's basically parenting 101 only administered by Cartman and the adults or children.

170
00:08:12,300 --> 00:08:13,819
Oh, and the consequences are severe.

171
00:08:14,180 --> 00:08:16,540
What we're gonna do, Butters, is we're gonna wait for your parents to leave the house

172
00:08:16,800 --> 00:08:19,240
and then smear all the walls with poop.

173
00:08:19,540 --> 00:08:21,040
I want you to take a look at some of these poop swatches.

174
00:08:21,379 --> 00:08:22,600
Poop comes in a lot of varieties, Butters.

175
00:08:22,660 --> 00:08:25,399
I want to find the perfect one tailored to your revenge on your parents.

176
00:08:25,540 --> 00:08:27,600
I mean, whatever happened in good old egging, right?

177
00:08:28,100 --> 00:08:30,500
Cartman suggests the same revenge plot for Stan's parents,

178
00:08:30,759 --> 00:08:32,360
but he doesn't think it'll work all too well.

179
00:08:32,559 --> 00:08:33,659
Dude, that's not extreme enough.

180
00:08:33,779 --> 00:08:36,340
My parents aren't gonna learn their lesson from having some crap smeared on their walls.

181
00:08:36,519 --> 00:08:37,139
Okay, okay, fine.

182
00:08:37,299 --> 00:08:38,639
I just, it's my first idea.

183
00:08:38,759 --> 00:08:39,399
That's why we have these consultations.

184
00:08:39,899 --> 00:08:41,340
I want them to see what they did was wrong.

185
00:08:41,460 --> 00:08:42,899
I want them to have to admit that they lied to me.

186
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:43,600
Oh, this is nice.

187
00:08:43,679 --> 00:08:43,940
How about this?

188
00:08:44,179 --> 00:08:46,220
We'll lure your parents out of the house and then we'll kill them.

189
00:08:46,500 --> 00:08:48,330
Uh, we'll cut them up into little pieces and feed them to the dog.

190
00:08:48,620 --> 00:08:50,019
Dude, that's not revenge.

191
00:08:50,340 --> 00:08:52,379
That's straight up murder, you little frackin' psychopath.

192
00:08:52,940 --> 00:08:54,899
In fairness, though, that is definitely more extreme.

193
00:08:55,200 --> 00:08:59,320
Anyway, Stan storms out accusing Carmen of not knowing what he's doing and thinking that he can just reason with

194
00:08:59,320 --> 00:09:01,740
his parents by having a direct and honest conversation with them.

195
00:09:02,009 --> 00:09:03,799
I don't think that guy is from the future.

196
00:09:04,179 --> 00:09:04,600
You don't?

197
00:09:04,870 --> 00:09:05,899
No. You know what I think?

198
00:09:06,299 --> 00:09:07,600
I think this is all an elaborate hoax.

199
00:09:07,879 --> 00:09:10,279
And I think that whoever is doing it doesn't have very much respect for me.

200
00:09:10,399 --> 00:09:13,279
See, the best way to try to motivate somebody is by being direct with them.

201
00:09:13,379 --> 00:09:14,139
To be honest with them.

202
00:09:14,460 --> 00:09:15,960
I think the whole future self thing is a lie.

203
00:09:16,120 --> 00:09:18,000
And lies are never the right way to get your message across.

204
00:09:18,519 --> 00:09:19,500
Well said, little Stan.

205
00:09:19,820 --> 00:09:21,779
Only... I think he is from the future.

206
00:09:22,220 --> 00:09:22,899
Yeah, he must be.

207
00:09:22,960 --> 00:09:24,980
It's like, I just feel he's our son, you know?

208
00:09:25,379 --> 00:09:26,559
Right, that's mother's intuition.

209
00:09:26,679 --> 00:09:27,500
You can't argue with that.

210
00:09:27,600 --> 00:09:29,019
It's like trying to talk to a tapestry.

211
00:09:29,500 --> 00:09:32,059
Stan then takes a more extreme approach and chops off his hand,

212
00:09:32,159 --> 00:09:34,539
arguing that his future self will also have a missing hand now.

213
00:09:34,840 --> 00:09:37,860
He is, however, greatly underestimating his dad's stubborn commitment.

214
00:09:39,820 --> 00:09:41,500
What? His hand did disappear.

215
00:09:41,600 --> 00:09:42,679
He is you from the future.

216
00:09:42,820 --> 00:09:42,879
Oh, God!

217
00:09:43,019 --> 00:09:43,919
Oh, Jesus!

218
00:09:44,179 --> 00:09:46,220
That's weird because I didn't really cut off my hand.

219
00:09:46,320 --> 00:09:46,720
It was fake.

220
00:09:48,039 --> 00:09:49,080
Oh, look!

221
00:09:49,259 --> 00:09:50,580
It was fake in the future, too!

222
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:51,639
Dang it.

223
00:09:51,899 --> 00:09:53,500
Also, never go into acting, kids.

224
00:09:53,720 --> 00:09:57,399
Meanwhile, over at Butters' house that's now smeared in dung thanks to Cartman and his crew.

225
00:09:57,700 --> 00:09:58,679
What the hell is this?

226
00:09:58,980 --> 00:09:59,720
Oh, my God!

227
00:09:59,840 --> 00:10:00,360
Our house!

228
00:10:00,659 --> 00:10:01,480
Oh, jeez.

229
00:10:01,600 --> 00:10:02,779
I'm gonna get it now.

230
00:10:03,019 --> 00:10:05,480
Butters! Do you have an explanation for this?

231
00:10:05,779 --> 00:10:06,519
Wait a minute, Chris.

232
00:10:07,019 --> 00:10:07,620
Don't you see?

233
00:10:07,899 --> 00:10:08,980
This might be our fault.

234
00:10:09,279 --> 00:10:10,320
My God, you're right, Linda.

235
00:10:10,639 --> 00:10:12,960
This is what we get for deceiving our son.

236
00:10:13,059 --> 00:10:15,590
So, you see, the parents of South Park are all certifiable morons.

237
00:10:15,879 --> 00:10:19,700
Butters' parents go on to confess that the whole future self thing was a ruse and that they were,

238
00:10:19,720 --> 00:10:22,919
in fact, wrong in their approach, which, of course, makes Cartman right.

